For 20 years, the smart money avoided hardware. It cost too much and moved too slowly. Now hardware is in fashion again, and that is exactly when a lawyer starts reading the fine print.  That tension drove the fifth Hard Things evening, which Foley & Lardner and Mavka Capital hosted on Nov. 2 at Helm.AI's offices. Helm.AI was the right room for this conversation. Founded in 2016 by CEO Vladislav Voroninski, it has become one of the leading physical AI companies, building the software that helps [...] What Technology Is Covered?

The law regulates “automated employment-related decision technology,” defined generally as technology that processes personal data and generates an output, such as a prediction, recommendation, classification, ranking, or score, that is a “substantial factor” in making or materially influencing an employment-related decision. A substantial factor is one that “meaningfully alters” the outcome of the decision. [...] that automated employment-related decision technology is being used;
 the technology’s purpose and the nature of the employment decision;
 the technology’s trade name;
 the categories and sources of personal data the technology will analyze as well as how the data will be assessed; and
 the employer’s contact information.

AI Is Not a Defense to Discrimination
